    "A grounded conductor is intentionally grounded and can carry normal load current. In common dwelling systems it is the neutral, but a grounded conductor is not always a neutral. The equipment grounding conductor bonds exposed metal and provides a fault-current path; it is not intended to carry normal load current.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A grounded conductor is intentionally grounded and can carry normal load current. In common dwelling systems it is the neutral, but a grounded conductor is not always a neutral. The equipment grounding conductor bonds exposed metal and provides a fault-current path; it is not intended to carry normal load current."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "A house has a range, a dryer, a water heater, a dishwasher and a disposal. How many appliances count toward the 75% demand factor for fastened-in-place appliances?": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A house has a range, a dryer, a water heater, a dishwasher and a disposal. How many appliances count toward the 75% demand factor for fastened-in-place appliances?"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "A load expected to run three hours or more is taken at 125%, and the multiplier applies to the conductor and to the device ahead of it: 44 × 1.25 = 55 A. The 50 A answer is the next standard device below the load, which is what you get by protecting at the load itself.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A load expected to run three hours or more is taken at 125%, and the multiplier applies to the conductor and to the device ahead of it: 44 × 1.25 = 55 A. The 50 A answer is the next standard device below the load, which is what you get by protecting at the load itself."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "A load is continuous if its maximum current is expected for three hours or more. Ordinary branch-circuit conductors and protection are sized for the noncontinuous load plus 125% of the continuous load. A listed assembly rated for continuous operation at 100% can qualify for an exception; check the equipment and the applicable rule.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A load is continuous if its maximum current is expected for three hours or more. Ordinary branch-circuit conductors and protection are sized for the noncontinuous load plus 125% of the continuous load. A listed assembly rated for continuous operation at 100% can qualify for an exception; check the equipment and the applicable rule."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "A load is continuous if its maximum current is expected to run for three hours or more. It matters because the branch circuit and its overcurrent device get sized at 125% of the continuous portion. Lighting in a store is the standard example; a household clothes dryer is not.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "A load is continuous if its maximum current is expected to run for three hours or more. It matters because the branch circuit and its overcurrent device get sized at 125% of the continuous portion. Lighting in a store is the standard example; a household clothes dryer is not."
          }
        }
      }
    },
