    "For a conductor connecting only to a rod, pipe or plate electrode, 250.66(A) does not require more than 6 AWG copper. This is a sizing permission, not a physical current limit of the electrode. The corresponding copper limit for a concrete-encased electrode is 4 AWG. For a ground ring it is the ring conductor's actual size.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For a conductor connecting only to a rod, pipe or plate electrode, 250.66(A) does not require more than 6 AWG copper. This is a sizing permission, not a physical current limit of the electrode. The corresponding copper limit for a concrete-encased electrode is 4 AWG. For a ground ring it is the ring conductor's actual size."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "For a raceway longer than 24 inches, count current-carrying conductors. Equipment grounds do not count. A neutral carrying only imbalance from the other conductors of the same circuit need not count. In a four-wire, three-phase wye circuit whose major load is nonlinear, the neutral does count. Check the exceptions before applying an adjustment.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For a raceway longer than 24 inches, count current-carrying conductors. Equipment grounds do not count. A neutral carrying only imbalance from the other conductors of the same circuit need not count. In a four-wire, three-phase wye circuit whose major load is nonlinear, the neutral does count. Check the exceptions before applying an adjustment."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "For an ordinary circuit with conductors not increased in size and no parallel raceways, what is the minimum wire equipment grounding conductor from Table 250.122?": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For an ordinary circuit with conductors not increased in size and no parallel raceways, what is the minimum wire equipment grounding conductor from Table 250.122?"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "For an ordinary continuous-duty motor over 1 hp using a separate current-responsive overload device under 430.32(A)(1), which current is the starting value?": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For an ordinary continuous-duty motor over 1 hp using a separate current-responsive overload device under 430.32(A)(1), which current is the starting value?"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "For an ordinary general-purpose circuit, with no correction, adjustment or specific 240.4(E)/(G) permission, what is the maximum overcurrent device on 10 AWG aluminum?": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For an ordinary general-purpose circuit, with no correction, adjustment or specific 240.4(E)/(G) permission, what is the maximum overcurrent device on 10 AWG aluminum?"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "For an ordinary single-speed motor of usual speed and torque characteristics, conductors and branch-circuit short-circuit protection use the code-table full-load current. Separate overload protection uses nameplate current. Low-speed, high-torque, multispeed and other specifically excepted motors require their own treatment under 430.6(A)(1).": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For an ordinary single-speed motor of usual speed and torque characteristics, conductors and branch-circuit short-circuit protection use the code-table full-load current. Separate overload protection uses nameplate current. Low-speed, high-torque, multispeed and other specifically excepted motors require their own treatment under 430.6(A)(1)."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "For an ordinary single-speed motor of usual speed and torque characteristics, what is the largest standard branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault device rating under the table and next-rating permission? Assume the selected device permits starting; do not apply the increased starting-current allowances.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For an ordinary single-speed motor of usual speed and torque characteristics, what is the largest standard branch-circuit short-circuit and ground-fault device rating under the table and next-rating permission? Assume the selected device permits starting; do not apply the increased starting-current allowances."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "For conductor sizing and for the branch-circuit short-circuit device, motor current comes from Tables 430.247 through 430.250, not from the nameplate. The nameplate value is used for one thing only: overload protection. Mixing those two up is the defining mistake in this article.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For conductor sizing and for the branch-circuit short-circuit device, motor current comes from Tables 430.247 through 430.250, not from the nameplate. The nameplate value is used for one thing only: overload protection. Mixing those two up is the defining mistake in this article."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "For ordinary general-purpose circuits, 240.4(D) limits 14, 12 and 10 AWG copper protection to 15, 20 and 30 A. Aluminum 12 and 10 AWG are limited to 15 and 25 A. Correction and adjustment can require still smaller protection. Specific permissions in 240.4(E) and (G), including motor circuits, can change these limits.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For ordinary general-purpose circuits, 240.4(D) limits 14, 12 and 10 AWG copper protection to 15, 20 and 30 A. Aluminum 12 and 10 AWG are limited to 15 and 25 A. Correction and adjustment can require still smaller protection. Specific permissions in 240.4(E) and (G), including motor circuits, can change these limits."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "For this single, ordinary single-speed continuous-duty motor of usual speed and torque characteristics, what minimum branch-circuit conductor ampacity does 430.22(A) require? Report the calculated minimum to the nearest 0.1 A.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "For this single, ordinary single-speed continuous-duty motor of usual speed and torque characteristics, what minimum branch-circuit conductor ampacity does 430.22(A) require? Report the calculated minimum to the nearest 0.1 A."
          }
        }
      }
    },
