      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The Chapter 5 rule"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The EGC never counts. That neutral carries only unbalanced current on a balanced linear load, so it does not count either. Three current-carrying conductors means no adjustment factor applies at all.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The EGC never counts. That neutral carries only unbalanced current on a balanced linear load, so it does not count either. Three current-carrying conductors means no adjustment factor applies at all."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The Five Shapes": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The Five Shapes"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The ampacity table assumes 30°C and no more than three current-carrying conductors. Hotter air multiplies the number down. Four or more current-carrying conductors multiplies it down again. Both apply together, and they multiply rather than add.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The ampacity table assumes 30°C and no more than three current-carrying conductors. Hotter air multiplies the number down. Four or more current-carrying conductors multiplies it down again. Both apply together, and they multiply rather than add."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The ampacity table says 14 AWG copper is good for 20 A in the 75°C column and 25 A at 90°C. It does not matter. Small conductors are capped at 15, 20 and 30 amps for 14, 12 and 10 AWG copper. Aluminum 12 and 10 cap at 15 and 25. This single rule accounts for more wrong answers than any other in the article.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The ampacity table says 14 AWG copper is good for 20 A in the 75°C column and 25 A at 90°C. It does not matter. Small conductors are capped at 15, 20 and 30 amps for 14, 12 and 10 AWG copper. Aluminum 12 and 10 cap at 15 and 25. This single rule accounts for more wrong answers than any other in the article."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The answer looks wrong": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The answer looks wrong"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The area is computed from the outside dimensions of the dwelling, and it excludes open porches, garages and unfinished spaces not adaptable for future use. An unfinished basement that could be finished is IN. That last part is where the square footage a question gives you and the square footage you should use diverge, and it is deliberate.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The area is computed from the outside dimensions of the dwelling, and it excludes open porches, garages and unfinished spaces not adaptable for future use. An unfinished basement that could be finished is IN. That last part is where the square footage a question gives you and the square footage you should use diverge, and it is deliberate."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The arithmetic came out below 100 A. A one-family dwelling service has a 100 A floor under 230.79(C), so that is the size.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The arithmetic came out below 100 A. A one-family dwelling service has a 100 A floor under 230.79(C), so that is the size."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The branch-circuit conductor ampacity": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The branch-circuit conductor ampacity"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The column is the insulation's rating, not the circuit's": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The column is the insulation's rating, not the circuit's"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The column you read is set by the conductor's insulation. The column you are allowed to finish in is set by whatever the conductor lands on: the breaker lugs, the terminal block, the equipment. Most equipment is rated 75°C, so most answers finish in the 75°C column no matter what the wire is rated.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The column you read is set by the conductor's insulation. The column you are allowed to finish in is set by whatever the conductor lands on: the breaker lugs, the terminal block, the equipment. Most equipment is rated 75°C, so most answers finish in the 75°C column no matter what the wire is rated."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The conductor can carry the 80 A load. Its 88 A allowable ampacity falls between standard ratings, and the stated 240.4(B) conditions permit the next rating, 90 A. Required load ampacity alone would not establish the conductor protection limit.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The conductor can carry the 80 A load. Its 88 A allowable ampacity falls between standard ratings, and the stated 240.4(B) conditions permit the next rating, 90 A. Required load ampacity alone would not establish the conductor protection limit."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The conductor itself: allowable ampacity, the temperature columns, ambient correction, and the adjustment for bundling more than three current-carrying conductors.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The conductor itself: allowable ampacity, the temperature columns, ambient correction, and the adjustment for bundling more than three current-carrying conductors."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The conductor to a ground rod": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The conductor to a ground rod"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The count excludes the ranges, dryers, space heating and air conditioning that have rules of their own, so a house with a range, a dryer, a water heater and a dishwasher has TWO appliances for this purpose, not four, and gets no discount. Counting the excluded ones into the group is what makes a candidate discount loads that should have stayed whole.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The count excludes the ranges, dryers, space heating and air conditioning that have rules of their own, so a house with a range, a dryer, a water heater and a dishwasher has TWO appliances for this purpose, not four, and gets no discount. Counting the excluded ones into the group is what makes a candidate discount loads that should have stayed whole."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The date changes what we put in front of you, not just what the countdown says. Sitting it this week gets a different plan from sitting it in the spring. You can skip it.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The date changes what we put in front of you, not just what the countdown says. Sitting it this week gets a different plan from sitting it in the spring. You can skip it."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The dedicated space is the column directly above the equipment footprint, running to six feet above the equipment or to the structural ceiling, whichever is lower, and foreign systems are excluded from it. It is a separate rule from the working space in front, which is where the fourth answer goes wrong.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The dedicated space is the column directly above the equipment footprint, running to six feet above the equipment or to the structural ceiling, whichever is lower, and foreign systems are excluded from it. It is a separate rule from the working space in front, which is where the fourth answer goes wrong."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The definition carries its own conditions: continuously, and under the conditions of use. That is why the same conductor has different ampacities in a hot attic and in a cool basement, and why bundling changes the number without changing the wire.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The definition carries its own conditions: continuously, and under the conditions of use. That is why the same conductor has different ampacities in a hot attic and in a cool basement, and why bundling changes the number without changing the wire."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The depth table is graded by condition: nothing live or grounded facing the equipment, grounded parts facing it, or live parts on both sides. Below 151 volts to ground all three land on the same three feet, so the grading only starts to bite on a 277/480 system, where the same three conditions run three, three and a half, and four feet. Candidates memorise the 480 volt row and then apply it to a house panel, which is the wrong direction to be wrong in.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The depth table is graded by condition: nothing live or grounded facing the equipment, grounded parts facing it, or live parts on both sides. Below 151 volts to ground all three land on the same three feet, so the grading only starts to bite on a 277/480 system, where the same three conditions run three, three and a half, and four feet. Candidates memorise the 480 volt row and then apply it to a house panel, which is the wrong direction to be wrong in."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The device yoke counts as two.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The device yoke counts as two."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The dryer is the greater of its nameplate and 5000 VA: %@ against 5000, so %@ VA.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The dryer is the greater of its nameplate and 5000 VA: %@ against 5000, so %@ VA."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The dryer load is 5000 VA or the nameplate, whichever is LARGER, so a small dryer is carried at 5000 and a 7200 VA dryer is carried at 7200. Reading it as a maximum rather than a minimum is the usual mistake, and it is one of the few places in Article 220 where the code rounds a load up rather than discounting it.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The dryer load is 5000 VA or the nameplate, whichever is LARGER, so a small dryer is carried at 5000 and a 7200 VA dryer is carried at 7200. Reading it as a maximum rather than a minimum is the usual mistake, and it is one of the few places in Article 220 where the code rounds a load up rather than discounting it."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The equipment grounding conductor comes from the overcurrent device rating in Table 250.122, and 200 A calls for 6 AWG copper. The conductor's own ampacity has nothing to do with it.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The equipment grounding conductor comes from the overcurrent device rating in Table 250.122, and 200 A calls for 6 AWG copper. The conductor's own ampacity has nothing to do with it."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The equipment grounding conductor is sized from the rating of the overcurrent device ahead of the circuit, not from the circuit conductors. That rating is %lld A.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The equipment grounding conductor is sized from the rating of the overcurrent device ahead of the circuit, not from the circuit conductors. That rating is %lld A."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The exam is open book": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The exam is open book"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The exam is testing whether you can work the method it named, not whether you can produce a safe installation. The two methods routinely disagree by a full service size, and the optional one is usually smaller, so the standard-method result is a wrong answer on that question even though it would be a legal installation.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The exam is testing whether you can work the method it named, not whether you can produce a safe installation. The two methods routinely disagree by a full service size, and the optional one is usually smaller, so the standard-method result is a wrong answer on that question even though it would be a legal installation."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The explanation is unclear": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The explanation is unclear"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The feeder or service neutral carries the maximum unbalance, so a straight 240 volt load with no neutral connection, such as a water heater or a resistance heater, contributes nothing to it. A range or a dryer has both 240 volt elements and 120 volt controls, so it counts at 70% of its calculated load. That 70% is the number to remember here.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The feeder or service neutral carries the maximum unbalance, so a straight 240 volt load with no neutral connection, such as a water heater or a resistance heater, contributes nothing to it. A range or a dryer has both 240 volt elements and 120 volt controls, so it counts at 70% of its calculated load. That 70% is the number to remember here."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The field calculation, drilled": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The field calculation, drilled"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The first 3000 VA at 100%, the rest up to 120,000 VA at 35%": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The first 3000 VA at 100%, the rest up to 120,000 VA at 35%"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The first 3000 VA stays whole and the remaining 8000 VA comes in at 35%: 3000 + 2800 = 5800 VA. Taking 35% of the whole 11,000 gives 3850, which is the answer you get by skipping the first band, and it is the second choice most people reach for.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The first 3000 VA stays whole and the remaining 8000 VA comes in at 35%: 3000 + 2800 = 5800 VA. Taking 35% of the whole 11,000 gives 3850, which is the answer you get by skipping the first band, and it is the second choice most people reach for."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The footprint of the equipment, from the floor to six feet above it or to the structural ceiling, whichever is lower": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The footprint of the equipment, from the floor to six feet above it or to the structural ceiling, whichever is lower"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The free rooms hold %lld questions, about %@ at %lld a day. That barely covers your %@, once, with nothing left to repeat.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
