    },
    "The full dwelling calculation": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The full dwelling calculation"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The full journeyman/electrician licence exam": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The full journeyman/electrician licence exam"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The general streets, roads, alleys, driveways and parking-lots row requires 24 inches. One- and two-family dwelling driveways used only for dwelling purposes have a separate row: 18 inches for the ordinary cable and raceway columns, 12 inches for the qualifying GFCI residential branch circuit, and 6 inches for qualifying low-voltage lighting. Concrete-cover rows and table notes must also be checked.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The general streets, roads, alleys, driveways and parking-lots row requires 24 inches. One- and two-family dwelling driveways used only for dwelling purposes have a separate row: 18 inches for the ordinary cable and raceway columns, 12 inches for the qualifying GFCI residential branch circuit, and 6 inches for qualifying low-voltage lighting. Concrete-cover rows and table notes must also be checked."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The general threshold is 2 feet, including measurements around corners. Fixed wall panels can count, while sliding panels and interruptions such as doorways, fireplaces and fixed cabinets are treated differently. A qualifying wall space needs coverage within 6 feet; it does not necessarily require a separate receptacle centered on each section.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The general threshold is 2 feet, including measurements around corners. Fixed wall panels can count, while sliding panels and interruptions such as doorways, fireplaces and fixed cabinets are treated differently. A qualifying wall space needs coverage within 6 feet; it does not necessarily require a separate receptacle centered on each section."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The general trigger is both 1200 A or more and over 6 feet wide, for equipment containing switching, overcurrent or control devices. Each entrance is at least 24 inches wide and 6.5 feet high. A single entrance can qualify where unobstructed egress or double working-space depth satisfies the specific alternatives in 110.26(C)(2).": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The general trigger is both 1200 A or more and over 6 feet wide, for equipment containing switching, overcurrent or control devices. Each entrance is at least 24 inches wide and 6.5 feet high. A single entrance can qualify where unobstructed egress or double working-space depth satisfies the specific alternatives in 110.26(C)(2)."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The grounded conductor is the neutral. It is a normal circuit conductor that happens to be connected to earth at the service, and it carries current every day. An equipment grounding conductor carries nothing until something goes wrong. Answers that treat them as interchangeable are always wrong.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The grounded conductor is the neutral. It is a normal circuit conductor that happens to be connected to earth at the service, and it carries current every day. An equipment grounding conductor carries nothing until something goes wrong. Answers that treat them as interchangeable are always wrong."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The grounding electrode conductor connects the system to earth and is sized from the service-entrance conductors. The equipment grounding conductor bonds metal parts back to the source and is sized from the rating of the overcurrent device ahead of it. Using the wrong table is the classic way to miss both questions in a pair.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The grounding electrode conductor connects the system to earth and is sized from the service-entrance conductors. The equipment grounding conductor bonds metal parts back to the source and is sized from the rating of the overcurrent device ahead of it. Using the wrong table is the classic way to miss both questions in a pair."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The headroom is clear space: piping, ducts and equipment foreign to the electrical installation are not allowed in it. The common exam framing is a ceiling at six feet, which fails, or a panel that is itself seven feet tall, where the equipment height governs and the answer is seven.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The headroom is clear space: piping, ducts and equipment foreign to the electrical installation are not allowed in it. The common exam framing is a ceiling at six feet, which fails, or a panel that is itself seven feet tall, where the equipment height governs and the answer is seven."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The larger of the two": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The larger of the two"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The larger one only, so 12 kW": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The larger one only, so 12 kW"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The largest standard rating in 240.6(A) at or below %lld A is %lld A.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The largest standard rating in 240.6(A) at or below %lld A is %lld A."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The list of standard fuse and breaker sizes, and the small-conductor ceiling that overrides the ampacity table for 14, 12 and 10 AWG.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The list of standard fuse and breaker sizes, and the small-conductor ceiling that overrides the ampacity table for 14, 12 and 10 AWG."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The longest question on the paper": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The longest question on the paper"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The motor nameplate full-load current": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The motor nameplate full-load current"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The nameplate %@ A is still used, but only for the separate overload device under 430.32.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The nameplate %@ A is still used, but only for the separate overload device under 430.32."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The nameplate is not the number here. Branch-circuit conductors for a single motor are sized from the full-load current in Table %@, which for a %@ motor at %@ is %@ A.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The nameplate is not the number here. Branch-circuit conductors for a single motor are sized from the full-load current in Table %@, which for a %@ motor at %@ is %@ A."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The nameplate says 28 amps": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The nameplate says 28 amps"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The neutral and the equipment grounding system connect at exactly one point: the service disconnect. Bonding them again in a subpanel puts normal load current onto the grounding conductors and every metal part they touch. Separately derived systems are the exception, and they get their own bonding point.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The neutral and the equipment grounding system connect at exactly one point: the service disconnect. Bonding them again in a subpanel puts normal load current onto the grounding conductors and every metal part they touch. Separately derived systems are the exception, and they get their own bonding point."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The neutral of a multiwire circuit that carries only unbalanced current is not counted. Two current-carrying conductors means no adjustment applies. The neutral is still a circuit conductor; it just does not count for this purpose.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The neutral of a multiwire circuit that carries only unbalanced current is not counted. Two current-carrying conductors means no adjustment applies. The neutral is still a circuit conductor; it just does not count for this purpose."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The next-size-up rule": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The next-size-up rule"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The one place you round up": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The one place you round up"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The optional calculation puts the general lighting, the small-appliance and laundry circuits, and the NAMEPLATE ratings of the range, dryer and every fastened-in-place appliance into one total, then applies one pair of percentages. Heating and air conditioning are added afterward at their own percentages. It usually lands on a smaller service than the standard method, which is why answering a question that names the optional method with a standard-method result is wrong rather than merely conservative.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The optional calculation puts the general lighting, the small-appliance and laundry circuits, and the NAMEPLATE ratings of the range, dryer and every fastened-in-place appliance into one total, then applies one pair of percentages. Heating and air conditioning are added afterward at their own percentages. It usually lands on a smaller service than the standard method, which is why answering a question that names the optional method with a standard-method result is wrong rather than merely conservative."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The optional method puts every general load in one pile, and it uses NAMEPLATE ratings: no column C for the range and no separate dryer minimum.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The optional method puts every general load in one pile, and it uses NAMEPLATE ratings: no column C for the range and no separate dryer minimum."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The problems that fail people": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The problems that fail people"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The question hands you square footage, appliance nameplates, or a list of loads and wants a service or feeder size. If you are adding VA together, you are in load calculations.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The question hands you square footage, appliance nameplates, or a list of loads and wants a service or feeder size. If you are adding VA together, you are in load calculations."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The question turns on what a word means rather than on a number. If you are arguing about whether something counts as a dwelling unit, a device, or readily accessible, the answer is in the definitions and nowhere else.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The question turns on what a word means rather than on a number. If you are arguing about whether something counts as a dwelling unit, a device, or readily accessible, the answer is in the definitions and nowhere else."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The questions you keep getting wrong": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The questions you keep getting wrong"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The raceway articles tell you how to install the pipe. They do not carry the fill math. Every conductor area, raceway area and fill percentage sits in Chapter 9. Annex C is the shortcut table for conductors that are all the same size, and it is informative rather than required.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The raceway articles tell you how to install the pipe. They do not carry the fill math. Every conductor area, raceway area and fill percentage sits in Chapter 9. Annex C is the shortcut table for conductors that are all the same size, and it is informative rather than required."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The range and the dryer are excluded because they have their own tables, leaving the water heater, dishwasher and disposal: three. Three is fewer than four, so no discount applies and all three go in whole. Counting the range and dryer to reach five is exactly the error the exclusion exists to catch.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The range and the dryer are excluded because they have their own tables, leaving the water heater, dishwasher and disposal: three. Three is fewer than four, so no discount applies and all three go in whole. Counting the range and dryer to reach five is exactly the error the exclusion exists to catch."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The range has its own table. %@ kW is not over 12 kW, so column C reads 8 kW flat: %@ VA.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The range has its own table. %@ kW is not over 12 kW, so column C reads 8 kW flat: %@ VA."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The range has its own table. %@ kW, which is over 12 kW, so column C's 8 kW rises 5%% per kW above 12: %@ VA.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The range has its own table. %@ kW, which is over 12 kW, so column C's 8 kW rises 5%% per kW above 12: %@ VA."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The rule that beats the table": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The rule that beats the table"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The rule that overrides the table": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The rule that overrides the table"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The rules that apply to EVERY method rather than to one: burial depth, protection from physical damage, conductors of different systems in the same enclosure, and sleeving through a wall. Reaching for the individual raceway article and not finding it is the tell that the answer is here.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The rules that apply to EVERY method rather than to one: burial depth, protection from physical damage, conductors of different systems in the same enclosure, and sleeving through a wall. Reaching for the individual raceway article and not finding it is the tell that the answer is here."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The same dwelling worked standard and optional, so the difference is visible": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The same dwelling worked standard and optional, so the difference is visible"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The same five questions for every member: two generated calculations and three drawn from across the rooms.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The same five questions for every member: two generated calculations and three drawn from across the rooms."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The same house, worked by the optional method for a one-family dwelling. What is the minimum standard service rating?": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The same house, worked by the optional method for a one-family dwelling. What is the minimum standard service rating?"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The same panel is a 120/240 V loadcentre in a garage with a concrete wall opposite. Minimum working depth?": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The same panel is a 120/240 V loadcentre in a garage with a concrete wall opposite. Minimum working depth?"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The same run is in PVC listed for direct burial, still in an open field. Minimum cover?": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The same run is in PVC listed for direct burial, still in an open field. Minimum cover?"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The size from the service conductors would be much larger, but the rod exception caps it: a conductor to a rod, pipe or plate electrode is never required to be larger than 6 AWG copper.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The size from the service conductors would be much larger, but the rod exception caps it: a conductor to a rod, pipe or plate electrode is never required to be larger than 6 AWG copper."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The small-conductor rule has separate aluminum values: 12 AWG aluminum caps at 15 A and 10 AWG aluminum at 25 A. Carrying the copper numbers over to aluminum is a fast way to miss this.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The small-conductor rule has separate aluminum values: 12 AWG aluminum caps at 15 A and 10 AWG aluminum at 25 A. Carrying the copper numbers over to aluminum is a fast way to miss this."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The standard method put the same house at 150 A. Both are correct calculations; only the one the question asked for is the right answer.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The standard method put the same house at 150 A. Both are correct calculations; only the one the question asked for is the right answer."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The standard ratings list and the rules about rounding to it are Article 240. Whether you may round up at all depends on what the circuit serves, which is also in 240.": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The standard ratings list and the rules about rounding to it are Article 240. Whether you may round up at all depends on what the circuit serves, which is also in 240."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "The table assumes 30°C": {
      "extractionState": "manual",
      "localizations": {
        "en": {
          "stringUnit": {
            "state": "translated",
            "value": "The table assumes 30°C"
